# ==========================================================================
# ChatE2EE -- files that must never be committed or put in an archive
# ==========================================================================
#
# THE NAME OF THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING.
#
# IT ALSO DISAPPEARS FROM ZIP ARCHIVES. Windows Explorer's "Send to >
# Compressed (zipped) folder" omits hidden files, and a dotfile is hidden on
# Windows. An archive round-trip through Explorer therefore deletes this file
# silently -- it has already happened once to this project, and the resulting
# tree looks completely normal while every rule below is absent. If you are
# rebuilding the repository from a zip, check for this file FIRST:
#
#     ls -a | grep gitignore
#
# tests/test_repo_hygiene.py fails loudly if it is missing, which is the
# cheapest available alarm.
#
# This content previously lived in a file called `gitignore`, with no leading
# dot. Git only reads `.gitignore`, so none of the rules below had any effect
# at all -- which is why pki/ (including ca.key), server/server.key,
# server/.venv/ (15 MB), *.db, __pycache__/, build-interop/ and interop.exe
# were still present in a 28 MB upload. Every rule needed was already written
# here. The file was simply never being read.
#
# There is no warning for this. Git does not report an unused ignore file, so
# the failure is completely silent and looks exactly like a working setup.
# Confirm it is live before trusting it:
#
#     git check-ignore -v pki/ca.key server/server.key
#
# Two lines of output naming this file means the rules are active. No output
# means they are not, and the keys are one `git add .` from being committed.
#
# The first section is the one that actually matters. Everything below it is
# ordinary build noise.


# ---- PRIVATE KEYS --------------------------------------------------------
#
# pki/ holds the lab certificate authority produced by tools/make_demo_pki.py.
# pki/ca.key is the key that can mint certificates this application will
# TRUST: anyone holding it can impersonate the relay to any build of the
# client that embeds the matching root. It is far more sensitive than the
# relay's own key, because re-issuing a leaf is routine whereas replacing the
# root means rebuilding and redistributing the client.
#
# The whole directory is excluded rather than just the .key files, because an
# accidental `git add pki/` is exactly the mistake this is here to prevent.
# The PUBLIC root is committed deliberately and separately, as
# client/pki/demo-ca.crt, which is the only piece the client needs.
pki/

# The relay's TLS private key. This has repeatedly ended up inside uploaded
# archives; if that has happened, rotate the key and reissue the certificate
# rather than assuming it went unnoticed.
#
# server/server.crt and server/server.key are an ORPHANED self-signed
# CN=localhost pair from 31 May. Nothing in the code references them: the
# client no longer forgives self-signed certificates (ignoreSslErrors was
# removed in favour of the lab CA), so that pair cannot be used for anything
# any more. Delete both from the working tree rather than relying on this
# rule -- an ignored file is still a file on your disk.
server/server.key
server/server.crt
*.key
*.pem

# Identity files: a user's long-term X25519/Ed25519 private identity.
*.ekey


# ---- Local data ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# SQLite databases hold message history and the relay's account table. They
# contain no plaintext message content (that is the point of the design), but
# they do contain account names, public keys and timing metadata.
#
# NOTE: SQLite runs in WAL mode here, so a database is only complete together
# with its -wal and -shm sidecars. If you ever deliberately share a database
# for debugging, checkpoint it first:  PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE);
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm


# ---- Build output --------------------------------------------------------
build/
build-*/
*.o
*.obj
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.a
*.apk
*.aab

# ---- REQUIRED BUILD INPUTS that the rules above would wrongly exclude ------
#
# libsodium cross-compiled for the Android ABIs. `*.a` above is a build-output
# rule, but these particular archives are build INPUT: nothing in this
# repository can regenerate them, because they come from an NDK cross-compile
# performed elsewhere. Excluded, a clean clone builds an Android package whose
# crypto is stubbed out -- CMake prints a WARNING nobody reads, the client
# registers a 32-byte-expected public key that is 0 bytes long, and the relay
# refuses the connection. The failure surfaces at login and looks nothing like
# a missing file.
#
# A negation must name the files, not just the directory: git does not descend
# into an excluded directory, so re-including a path inside one has no effect.
# These lines therefore re-include the archives and the headers explicitly.
#
# libsodium is ISC-licensed, so shipping the compiled archive is permitted.
!client/third_party/libsodium/**/*.a
!client/third_party/libsodium/include/**

# Qt / CMake generated
moc_*.cpp
moc_*.h
qrc_*.cpp
ui_*.h
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
cmake_install.cmake
Makefile
.qtcreator/
*.user
*.user.*


# ---- Python --------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/


# ---- Qt Creator per-machine state ----------------------------------------
#
# .qtcreator/ holds absolute paths and kit identifiers from ONE machine. It is
# actively harmful to share: a classmate opening the project inherits build
# directories that do not exist and kit ids that mean nothing on their system,
# and Qt Creator then reports a configuration error rather than simply asking
# them to choose a kit.
client/.qtcreator/


# ---- Editor / OS ---------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
